WEBVTT

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:20.308
This is Allie Brudney and today is March 21, 2012.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.440
I am here at the University of Wisconsin in Madison with Tony Michaels, and we are going

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.140
to record an interview as part of the Yiddish Book Center's Wexler Oral History Project.

00:00:28.444 --> 00:00:29.944
Tony, do I have your permission to record?

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.040
this interview. Yes.

00:00:31.376 --> 00:00:32.216
Thank you.

00:00:32.428 --> 00:00:34.468
So I want to just start.

00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:37.468
Could you briefly tell me about your family background?

00:00:38.412 --> 00:00:40.632
Well, I was born in.

00:00:42.412 --> 00:00:43.972
Uh, how much, all right, this is.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.740
When you say family background, do you talk about parents?

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.660
Where did your parents come from or your grandparents?

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.060
Okay. My parents were both born in Chicago.

00:00:53.836 --> 00:00:54.796
Uh, my.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.560
Grandparents, my maternal grandparents came from.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Ohio. My paternal grandparents, one, my paternal grandmother was born in Russia, came to the United

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.730
States at a very, very young age. She used to deny that she was born in Russia until she wasn't able

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:14.890
to.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.480
denied anymore because the information came out. Actually, I think she still continued

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:25.700
to deny it, even after the information came out. My paternal grandfather was born in the

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.960
United States, I think raised in Chicago. Eventually my...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.900
All my grandparents converged in Chicago.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.520
That's where, as I say, my parents grew up.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.660
How did your grandmother deny?

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.140
She just said she was born in the United States.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.050
And how did the information come out?

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:47.870
If I remember correctly, it had to do, it came out after my father applied for a job,

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.850
and I think a background check was done.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.580
So do you know anything about your parents' growing lives in Chicago?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:00.360
before.

00:02:00.796 --> 00:02:03.996
Sure, my father grew up on the west side of Chicago.

00:02:05.468 --> 00:02:09.888
Father owned a shoe store in Cicero, which is actually outside of Chicago, just to the west.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.940
And my mother grew up in the northwest part of Chicago.

00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:17.984
This is in the 1940s and 50s.

00:02:18.496 --> 00:02:23.136
And my father's family did eventually move to the northwest side and they met in high school.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:25.780
Um,

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:26.660
So I guess.

00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:28.652
Want to move to your childhood?

00:02:29.172 --> 00:02:29.292
Sure.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.960
So would you say that you grew up in a Jewish home with your house?

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.460
I grew up in a Jewish home.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.330
I grew up in a Jewish home.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.430
I grew up in a Jewish home, yes.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.180
It wasn't especially, it was not an especially religious home.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:59.970
There were various Jewish artifacts, books, a menorah here or there, some Jewish artifacts.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.540
artwork.

00:03:01.372 --> 00:03:06.552
But it was not a strong part of the day-to-day atmosphere of my childhood.

00:03:06.812 --> 00:03:07.812
with your family.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:08.500
Um,

00:03:08.892 --> 00:03:10.132
part of a synagogue.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:11.620
We were members of synagogues, yeah.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.640
Like most Jews, they joined synagogues and, you know,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:17.960
we moved around a lot.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.660
So that's why I say we joined synagogues.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.220
Wherever we lived, we joined a synagogue

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.620
and then did not attend, I think was the...

00:03:25.756 --> 00:03:29.396
is the standard pattern for most American Jews at that time anyway.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.800
But we did belong, usually to conservative synagogue, sometimes to reform.

00:03:35.376 --> 00:03:38.116
And I went to Hebrew school.

00:03:39.084 --> 00:03:40.844
you know, became a bar mitzvah at 13.

00:03:41.164 --> 00:03:43.224
Could you describe what your bar mitzvah was like?

00:03:44.172 --> 00:03:45.052
The bar mitzvah was fun.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.580
I hated Hebrew school, absolutely.

00:03:48.204 --> 00:03:52.484
load it, at least as I got older. Eventually I dropped out.

00:03:53.196 --> 00:03:54.136
And, um,

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.560
My mom hired a tutor.

00:03:56.108 --> 00:03:59.988
For some reason, even though I hated Hebrew school, I still wanted to become a barman.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.750
And so she hired a tutor who was actually an Indian Jewish woman, which I didn't think

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.890
much of at the time, but now looking back on it, it's unusual, especially where I was

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:16.990
raised in California, where there weren't many Jews in the part of California I'm from.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.090
So in any case, I had a tutor.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:23.080
I learned a lot from her, and that led towards my bar mitzvah.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:28.530
The event itself was not especially a major event in my life.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:39.880
Well, I'd say it actually was a major event in my life

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.880
in as much as it signaled the end of Hebrew school

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.780
and preparing for my bar mitzvah.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.380
In that sense, it was a wonderful change in my life.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:51.260
But beyond that, the bar mitzvah itself didn't mean a whole lot.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.680
It was a fun party, you know.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:59.060
So I saw that you wrote an article on Tablet about surfing.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:04:59.540
Oh, yes.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.310
Could you, would you mind talking about that a little bit?

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:04.010
No, no.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.400
No, I've already gone public, so it's okay.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.340
Well, so I started surfing just after my bar mitzvah,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.960
and I used bar mitzvah money I'd received from my bar mitzvah,

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.580
$80 to be exact, to buy my first surfboard,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.400
and it was something my friends and I just started doing

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.360
all together at the same time.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.700
And, and, uh,

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.520
In a way, it signaled the transition, you know, not of going into adulthood as a Jew,

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:41.620
but as leaving the Jewish, you know, my Jewish obligations, let's say, behind.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.740
Although I say it's an obligation, the same thing I did want, as I said,

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.910
I wanted to become a bar mitzvah, so I guess I had some mixed feelings about it.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.050
But what it did mean is I didn't have to go to Hebrew school anymore, and that was wonderful.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.070
And so I started surfing, and that was fun too.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.640
So that became my major for the next.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:09.800
At least until I went to college, that was the main, my main interest, I'd say, and my main interest in life was surfing.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:17.000
Not the only interest. I mean, I loved other things, too, music and reading to some extent, and had, of course, a social life.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:24.460
But that was my main passion, was surfing, which I came to have some understanding of as being a very un-Jewish thing to do.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:29.680
And apparently, at least some of my friends thought so as well, because at least one of them.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.720
made a point, made a point of saying that. I think I must have been about 17.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:36.800
What did he say?

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:48.010
He asked me in a sort of, he asked me with a chuckle whether there were any Jewish pro surfers.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:55.200
You know, obviously it was not a sincere question. And there was one I could think of,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.120
a very famous Jewish pro surfer, but his name was Sean Thompson, who's South African.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.980
I knew, you know, I mean, I knew my friend was basically right.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.140
There was only one.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.220
And he happened to be a great one, one of the best of all time.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.240
So I kind of scored a point by mentioning Sean Thompson's name.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.620
And he was, my friend, of course, was surprised.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.380
But he was basically the one who was right.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:21.880
There weren't many Jews in pro-surfing or surfing generally.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.980
It just wasn't something many Jews did.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.300
And I had that feeling.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:28.420
My parents had that feeling.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:29.440
Apparently everyone did.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.180
to some degree or another uh and i suppose i still feel that way on some level that it's kind

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:42.990
of a goyish activity uh but but i still love it um so i want to just ask a couple more questions

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:48.820
about your childhood were there did your family celebrate holidays are there any ones that stick

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:56.430
out in your mind oh we did but half-heartedly um and probably less so over time as i got older so

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.710
We went to shul on the high holidays, but you know, sometimes

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.800
My dad went off, and he didn't go.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.100
My mom went.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.200
We usually showed up late.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.220
I don't think anyone's hearts were in it.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:16.260
We had, you know, seders, but, again, they usually weren't especially elaborate.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.910
They weren't rigorous.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.350
They weren't festive.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:25.270
They weren't just, we were usually going through the motions, I'd say, more than anything else.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:29.910
I'd say that was, I was younger in Hebrew school for that reason.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:37.350
And my parents would take me to, say, a Purim celebration, a Purim carnival or something like that.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:47.100
So I do have definitely recollections of going to some sort of Purim party or a Hanukkah party.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:51.480
But they don't stand out as major parts of my...

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:58.380
My recollection was I had always seen that my family was the family that was among the least involved

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.900
and the other kids' families.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.560
were more involved. That was my feeling, that we didn't really celebrate perhaps the way

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.670
we should or certainly the way the others did. That's my recollection.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:20.820
So how did you go from this childhood of sort of being half-involved to graduate school

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:21.480
and to your studies?

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:29.480
That's a hard question to answer. It started in college.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:35.250
I went to University of California, Santa Cruz, and it started in college.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.070
It's hard to explain why.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:42.450
I mean, part of the reason, I suppose, had to do with the general atmosphere of UC Santa Cruz,

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:50.770
which encouraged interest in ethnic identity, because multiculturalism was big at the time on many college campuses,

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:52.810
but especially UC Santa Cruz's campus.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:59.010
So there was an atmosphere that encouraged that sort of thing, even though there was no Jewish studies program.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.960
So there weren't many outlets to pursue it in an academic sense.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:06.260
There were a few classes in literature, a class in the Holocaust.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:12.420
But there weren't many outlets.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.060
But there was an atmosphere that fostered that interest or encouraged it.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:22.270
And there were certainly a lot of other Jewish students, I'd say, like me in that sense,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:27.210
that were developing a curiosity in Jewish identity, let's call it.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.600
And there were extracurricular ways to do that.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.300
There was a Jewish student newspaper that I was involved in.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.870
We started a Jewish student group at one point, a political group.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.370
It was called the Progressive Jewish Student Union.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:48.990
We weren't a union, but I think we must have liked the connotation of that word.

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:50.710
So we did that.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:55.730
I think it was our third or fourth year, my third or fourth year in college.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.660
It lasted for a number of years, I know that, and a number of people in Jewish studies.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.320
today who went to UC Santa Cruz are actually involved in the organization.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:09.520
And I think that the common feeling was that Jewish students needed some sort of political

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:12.100
group that fit their identities.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:18.940
Most of us you could describe as being politically on the left, but friendly towards Israel.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.520
And I think that combination is what we felt was missing on campus.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:28.280
This was during the first intifada, so the Middle East was kind of a hot issue.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:28.360
to.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:29.380
And.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:35.730
And I'd say most people who joined the group had that more or less that combination of feelings.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:39.870
Not that we all had the same opinions, but that was our orientation.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.370
Critical of Israel, but sympathetic to Israel.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:52.080
And also having some strong interest in Jewish identity.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:58.280
There were feminists, there were people who came from other political organizations on campus.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:11:59.960
Some maybe had been involved in the...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.300
anti-apartheid movement, maybe some had been involved in the Jesse Jackson

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:08.160
campaign, maybe some of us were involved in this Jewish student newspaper, whatever.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:12.920
There was a sort of a hodgepodge, but we were friends and we had this, like I said,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:13.640
shared orientation.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.640
What was the focus of the Jewish student newspaper, what kind of articles?

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:28.660
The newspaper published articles on all kinds of things, Israel, literature, culture, some

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:29.360
history

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:34.880
It came out quarterly, and so there was a lot of space in it

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.920
for lengthy articles of one kind or another.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.740
Oh, man, now I can't remember specific articles.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.820
I remember an article on a professor of Sephardic literature

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:50.820
named Victor Pereira who taught at UC Santa Cruz.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:51.980
He had just published a book.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:53.820
I remember someone wrote a piece on him.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:59.180
Someone wrote a piece, I think, on the controversy involving Paul DeMond.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.780
who was at the time an important figure in English literature,

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.380
and he had Nazi ties or something to that effect.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:11.870
Going back to the World War II period, I think someone wrote a piece on that.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:18.360
So I recall an interview with a professor on campus,

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:23.990
Bettina Abdecker, who was Jewish and a very important person in the women's studies program.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:25.590
She came from a communist family.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.870
I remember she was interviewed once.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:29.510
There was an inter...

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:29.910
Oh, I remember.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.360
One time there was a debate on whether or not a Jewish fraternity should be established on campus.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.080
That was a really controversial issue.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:43.520
And so the person who wanted to establish the fraternity wrote an article,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:44.880
and then there was an opposing article.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.140
In the end, he didn't start the fraternity.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.140
He faced a lot of pressure.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.400
There was a lot of opposition among students, and it was not established.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.900
Maybe it was later, in later years, but not while I was there.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:59.860
and that particular student became

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.660
became head of the Dukakis campaign for president instead, which I think probably endeared him to

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:14.040
more students than the fraternity. Although maybe not, because Dukakis was on the conservative end

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:20.440
of, you know, maybe it didn't help his fortunes, his social life on campus. I don't know. I didn't

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:27.570
know him. But anyway, there was an article, there was a debate on that. What else was there? I don't

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.960
You know, book reviews, other things.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:34.660
Are there any classes that you took that were especially influential?

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:41.220
And pointing me towards Jewish studies? I wouldn't say there were any classes that did

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:46.280
that. It's hard to explain. I had the interest already, so I chose certain classes that allowed

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:51.300
me to learn more about Jewish history. So I took a course on the history of the Holocaust

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:58.700
with Peter Kinez and Murray Baumgarten. I took two courses on Middle East politics.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:14:59.980
That was one of my...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.720
main intersection in Middle East politics. I was a politics major, and it was one of

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:14.120
my, you know, preoccupations, for sure, was that. So I took that. I took a course in, is

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:17.740
there anything that counted as Jewish? Well, I actually went to UC Santa Barbara for two

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:21.910
quarters. I left Santa Cruz, and there I took a course on the history of Jerusalem, which

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:28.270
I loved. I took a course on the history of anti-Semitism, which was very, it was great

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:28.990
interest to me.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.600
And those were, you know, Jewish studies courses that I took, you know.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:39.560
Not a whole lot, but they were some of my favorite courses.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:46.340
And then I took courses in other subjects that I was able to work in a kind of Jewish interest into.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:47.980
So I took a course in the history of McCarthyism.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:54.040
And I think I wrote on the Hollywood Blacklist.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.460
And, of course, there's a Jewish dimension to that.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.180
So I remember doing that.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:15:59.980
Well, most of my coursework didn't have anything to do with it.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.830
Jews or Jewish subjects. I had other interests, history of labor and working class movements,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:11.970
history of socialism, international affairs, the Cold War. These were some of my major interests

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:19.310
at the time. And so the Jewish dimension was really a hobby for the most part. And I

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:24.290
read a lot on my own. And it was something of great personal interest. It was something

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:29.390
I involved with in campus life. And that was it. Your original question was how did

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:29.970
should I get involved in?

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.260
And I suppose it's hard to put my finger on it.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:33.540
It was, again, part of the atmosphere.

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:35.280
I don't know beyond that why.

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:39.160
I can't exactly tell you why around the time I was 18 or so,

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:44.990
it became an interest after five years or so when it really wasn't.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:50.110
So when did you start and how did you and why did you start learning Yiddish?

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.370
Sorry, if I can say one more thing.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:56.450
I was also around a lot of Jewish students for the first time in my life.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:57.850
That was also a difference.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.970
My dorm, I think, was about...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.340
50 percent Jewish and that was something I wasn't expecting when I came to Santa

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:09.860
Cruz and but I liked it I enjoyed it there were a lot of very quirky

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:16.530
individuals who I became friends with and had a lot in common with even though our

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.470
