members had to participate in education and cultural activities. And that was typical of

00:35:09.090 --> 00:35:13.770
socialist groups at the time, to emphasize education and culture. And so the Workmen's

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:18.450
Circle did that, and it grew very quickly. I think by 1910, there were almost 40,000 members.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:24.450
And it grew quickly because it filled such a need in the lives of immigrants, especially

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:29.400
those sick and death benefits. You know, there was no welfare state at the time. The unions

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:29.960
were very weak.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.420
weak and unstable in the 1890s and 1900s. So if you were unemployed, if you injured

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:40.580
yourself on the job, something like that, you had very few places to turn to. So the

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:43.940
workman's circle was a place you could go. You paid your benefits, you paid your monthly

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:49.220
dues. Sorry, not benefits, you paid your dues. And so if you hurt yourself, then you could

00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:56.400
collect your benefits and not starve. It was very practical, very useful, and there

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:59.920
there was a strong need for it and, and then, um, and.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:06.020
And for those workers who wanted to be educated or receive some cultural elevation and education

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:12.340
that had that benefit because they organized lectures and other sorts of educational activities.

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:17.040
Eventually the Workman's Circle started a whole national education network.

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:20.410
Lecture tours, for instance.

00:36:21.150 --> 00:36:22.370
Correspondence courses.

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:25.330
Schools for children in Yiddish and English.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:29.700
A book publishing concern.

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:35.270
all sorts of things. So it was really as much an educational and cultural organization as it was

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:41.260
a mutual aid society. What do you think the role of the workman's circle is today? I have no idea,

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:47.220
actually. I have no idea. I hope it, I know it exists, and there are a number of branches on

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:51.540
the East Coast, I think the West Coast too, and maybe elsewhere. None, there isn't one in Madison,

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:56.880
Wisconsin. Maybe I should start one, I don't know, but, but I hope it does well. That's all I can

00:36:56.880 --> 00:36:59.980
to say. I hope it does really well, but I don't know what role it is.

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:07.390
Yeah. How do you think that the history of Jewish socialism is relevant today?

00:37:07.590 --> 00:37:21.220
Oh, how is it relevant today? That's a good question. Well, on one level you can say

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:27.300
that there's a legacy, increasingly probably a tenuous legacy, but there is inasmuch as

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:29.980
so many American Jews come from families that have lived in the United States of America

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.160
that were involved in some way or another in the socialist labor movement, in some

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:40.280
way or another, meaning that they weren't necessarily hardcore activists, but were involved

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:45.580
in one or more of the institutions associated with the Jewish labor movement, and as much

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:54.000
as that's true, it has left certain legacies, and one has been over the years an orientation

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:57.940
towards liberal, progressive, left-wing causes.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.220
That's weakened over the decades, but it's still there.

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:09.100
It still has some presence in the lives of, I think, a significant number of Jews.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:10.600
I see it in my courses, actually.

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:14.900
I have students who come in, and they don't think of themselves as socialists.

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.240
They might not even think of themselves as liberals.

00:38:16.340 --> 00:38:22.740
I don't know, but they know where their parents and grandparents came from,

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:26.180
and that has some meaning for them.

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:28.640
And this happens every semester.

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:29.980
They don't know much.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.840
about the workmen's circle, they don't know much about the International Ladies Garment

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:36.740
Workers Union, they don't know much about the, I don't know, you know, the forwards.

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:40.840
But they know they existed, they know their parents or grandparents were part of that.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:46.500
And when they learn more about it, they do tend to develop an interest in it as well,

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:51.710
because this is where family history merges with a larger story of American history and

00:38:51.710 --> 00:38:52.250
Jewish history.

00:38:52.490 --> 00:38:56.750
So I think it's there, I think it informs to some degree, it's in the background, and

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:59.630
And I think it informs, to some degree, how Jews use it.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:08.880
themselves politically, to some degree view themselves as Jews, which is to say their Jewish

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:14.600
identity is some weakened version of that older Jewish identity, which is meaning that

00:39:14.600 --> 00:39:19.060
they have some kind of secular or cultural identification with Jews, maybe an ethnic sense

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:25.700
of being Jewish. Religion is not that important to them. But that's, you know, who they are

00:39:26.450 --> 00:39:29.020
It's like I said, some sort of, some sort of...

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.660
version of what was handed down to them from the immigrant generation.

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:38.040
You know, critics say this isn't sustainable, it's weak, it's inauthentic.

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:41.840
There's many critics of it, and I suppose you can, you know, to some degree or another,

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:48.500
you could say these are valid in one way or another, but, you know, I'm not judgmental

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:48.940
about it.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:55.160
I think there's actually that's positive in that legacy, and in fact, I'd like to see

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:56.080
the legacy strengthened.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:59.980
strengthened. The unfortunate thing about it.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.840
is that the kind of Yiddish, secular, left-wing identity I write about in my book

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:09.740
was difficult to sustain because there were built in tensions within it from the start.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:13.100
There was a tension between the universalism of socialism.

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:17.660
It's not an ethnically particular ideology.

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:19.440
So there was that.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:24.200
There was, so there's that tension between socialism and universalism

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:29.970
and the Jewishness, the Jewish culture of this movement.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.420
At the same time, this Jewish culture was difficult to pass on because it lacked a religious

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:35.000
dimension.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:40.840
It was built so much on a foreign language, Yiddish, but Yiddish, foreign languages don't

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:42.940
fare well generally in the United States.

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:47.480
They tend to wither away with successive generations.

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:53.190
So there was an unstable, there was instability built into this culture.

00:40:53.350 --> 00:40:55.330
It was also an incredibly creative culture.

00:40:55.330 --> 00:40:59.170
And so I don't fault it.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.700
In other words, much of American Jewish life contains that instability, the dynamism that

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:17.040
comes from living in a culture and a society that has a lot going on and in which traditions

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:23.280
are always being undermined and recreated and reinvented and new forms of culture created

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:26.160
that may not last but are vibrant in their own moment.

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:27.840
Traditionalists don't like this.

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:29.900
I mean, they look for stability in cotton.

00:41:30.820 --> 00:41:46.520
I think stability and continuity, well, I don't know. I think there's a cost to pursuing continuity above all else.

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:53.080
There's just as much a cost in doing that as embracing innovation and change and so forth.

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:59.900
And the cost is conformity.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:05.480
The cost can be something that's creating something that's actually completely uninteresting and boring.

00:42:07.240 --> 00:42:10.920
There are advantages as well, and tradition has its own advantages.

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:17.240
I'm saying that various, what I'm trying to say is that different paths and choices Jews have made come with costs and benefits.

00:42:17.640 --> 00:42:23.430
Yiddish culture came out of a tremendous cultural, secular Yiddish culture of the immigrant period.

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:29.960
It was a product of tremendous ferment and...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:38.930
rebellion and an embrace of modernity. And at the same time, those very elements of modernity

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:47.860
undermined it. And that to me is the sort of the situation. What I guess I'm trying to

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:52.520
say is even though there's this unfortunate in certain regards, because I wish Yiddish

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:57.860
culture was more stable in this country than it is, that it's also, I believe, the

00:42:57.860 --> 00:42:59.880
condition of modernity and I don't know how

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:06.620
escape that. Orthodox Jews try to escape that. Many Orthodox Jews do, but they can't even.

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:07.510
No one can.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:12.630
So we're sort of running out of time, but I have a few more questions. You mentioned

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:20.500
teaching and how you have some Jewish students. How diverse are your classes in Jewish history

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:23.660
and what's it like to teach various student populations?

00:43:25.030 --> 00:43:29.970
I'd say moderately diverse, meaning that I'd say about my service.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:35.310
survey course on American Jewish history probably gets about, I don't know, 60 to 70% of the

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:41.190
students are Jews. That's pretty good, I'd say, to have 30% or so that aren't. I think that's

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:46.490
pretty good. 50-50 might be better, but, you know, I'm pretty happy with that. Among the

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:51.830
students who aren't Jews, not a lot of diversity, because this campus isn't especially diverse

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:58.860
ethnically or religiously. Well, I suppose you can say it's religiously diverse. I've

00:43:58.860 --> 00:43:59.960
had Calvinists, I've had

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:06.100
Lutherans. I've had Catholics. So you could say it's somewhat diverse. But it's not

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:09.900
ethnic, it's especially ethnically diverse. So, so I, you know, I have some, maybe one

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.640
or two Asian American students occasionally, and occasionally African American students

00:44:13.640 --> 00:44:17.280
and a couple Latinos. If I taught in California, I think the student body would be very different.

00:44:17.930 --> 00:44:22.640
What's it like to teach a class with both Jews who have some personal connection and

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:25.380
people, even Jews who have less of a personal connection?

00:44:25.380 --> 00:44:27.040
You know, I don't think about it that much anymore.

00:44:33.230 --> 00:44:41.520
I would say, often, among the best students, among those students who are sort of at the

00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:47.010
top of the class, it's usually the case that non-Jewish students are well represented among

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:53.160
those. And that's because they're in the class, they're motivated to be in the class,

00:44:53.440 --> 00:44:59.640
I think. Now, among those students, they could be there for any number of reasons. Some might

00:44:59.640 --> 00:44:59.840
be

00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:08.390
devout Christians who want to learn about the chosen people. And they, those students

00:45:08.390 --> 00:45:16.190
can ask very challenging and interesting questions. Another, other time you get non-Jewish students

00:45:16.190 --> 00:45:23.080
who are dating Jews and want to learn about their boyfriend or girlfriend's culture. And

00:45:23.080 --> 00:45:27.540
they can also be very motivated and that's that's good too and Jewish students

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:41.040
So one of the challenges, and I don't know if this is more prevalent among Jewish students

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:44.140
or non-Jewish students, I'm not sure about this, is that they don't necessarily treat

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:47.520
the subject they think as seriously as some other subjects.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:53.760
This is, I think there's some feeling that this is an easy subject or that they know

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:59.940
much, easy because it's something they already know or they think they know a lot about.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:07.510
out. So one challenge, actually, is dealing with that, and the subjects that are more

00:46:07.510 --> 00:46:12.110
likely to be of interest to them, or they come with an interest in subjects, are, you

00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:25.570
know, religion, the Holocaust, to some degree Israel, anti-Semitism, summer camps, you know,

00:46:25.570 --> 00:46:29.970
Things they know are most familiar with synagogue life and so forth.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:34.140
social history of Jews doesn't tend to interest them as much political

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:39.240
movements things that I write about do research on that appeals to a small

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:47.180
number interestingly I wish it appealed to more it's it's a certain type of

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:51.960
student that wants to know about Yiddish or wants to know about the labor

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:57.870
movement or wants even to know about Israel in a serious way those do tend to

00:46:57.870 --> 00:46:59.930
be the more serious students and I say that because

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.520
because it takes more, it's more of a mental stretch for them to get that this is not the

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:10.940
subjects they're immediately familiar with. So they, you know, all the Jewish students

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:16.180
have some awareness of Yiddish on some vague level. Most don't have a real curiosity in

00:47:16.180 --> 00:47:20.780
it. So to be curious about something that you're not familiar with means already that you want

00:47:20.780 --> 00:47:27.450
to learn, where there are students who want to just in some way, again, if they want to

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.150
I just hear about the summer camp experiences.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:35.180
not especially challenging. Now, having said that, I don't mean to give you the impression

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:40.180
that there aren't a lot of serious students who are Jews and non-Jews. There are. And

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:45.900
so, you know, I guess the main, the main, my main answer to your question is that I like

00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:50.580
teaching the mix that I have. To the extent I have a mix, it's a wonderful, it's a wonderful

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:59.040
experience. If there's a, you know, the challenge is, I suppose, getting them to think about

00:47:59.040 --> 00:47:59.840
Jews in

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:05.290
ways they're unaccustomed to doing. Here's just one other challenge. American Jews in

00:48:05.290 --> 00:48:09.090
the 21st century are very different from American Jews a hundred years ago, and it's very hard

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:16.950
I think for many to wrap their minds around that, that they spoke a different language,

00:48:17.150 --> 00:48:21.330
and that Judaism was different, and they might not have cared about Judaism at all.

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:27.500
And this was before the creation of the State of Israel, and there was this Yiddish culture,

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:29.760
or there was a, or, or there was a,

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.880
let's say, a richer Jewish civilization than they're familiar with.

00:48:33.160 --> 00:48:38.440
All that stuff, I think, is so different to what they know as Jews.

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:41.960
The fact that Jews might not have called themselves,

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:44.120
that the Jews call themselves by different names,

00:48:44.180 --> 00:48:47.880
a race, a people, a nation, you know, an ethnicity,

00:48:48.220 --> 00:48:53.100
all these terms and concepts are so unfamiliar.

00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:56.080
So the challenge is getting students to think about Jews

00:48:56.080 --> 00:48:57.380
in different ways than they know.

00:48:57.690 --> 00:48:59.920
So we're

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.740
What role do you think that academics play or don't in the transmission of culture?

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:07.210
That's a good question.

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:10.540
I'm not sure the answer.

00:49:10.680 --> 00:49:17.660
I can answer, certainly I can answer for myself, which is that it was through study

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:22.580
of history, or let's say study of Jews in an academic sense, history, literature, culture

00:49:22.580 --> 00:49:29.160
and so forth, that, that enabled me to develop an interest in Jewish things.

00:49:29.160 --> 00:49:29.620
without

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:33.520
Without Jewish studies, then I don't know what my interest would have been,

00:49:33.580 --> 00:49:36.240
because it certainly wasn't the synagogue, as I mentioned before.

00:49:36.980 --> 00:49:38.500
It could have been Israel, I suppose,

00:49:38.820 --> 00:49:42.170
but given that I probably wasn't going to move to Israel,

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:45.990
then I don't know if that could have played any meaningful day-to-day role in my life.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:48.440
So I don't know.

00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:51.420
For me, Jewish studies has always been, in my adult life,

00:49:51.820 --> 00:49:58.700
the primary scholarship, and scholarship is the primary conduit,

00:49:58.740 --> 00:49:59.960
as opposed to Jewish civilization.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.700
That's my connection to it, and that's what interests me.

00:50:05.900 --> 00:50:10.140
So if your question is, do I play any role in transmitting that, I hope so.

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:11.800
I can't answer my students.

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:12.980
I don't know what they think.

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:19.920
I mean, I do have some sense that some small number of students respond to Jewish studies

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.800
courses, some of my students, and they'll take more.

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:25.560
And I say that because they'll take other Jewish studies courses.

00:50:25.560 --> 00:50:28.280
They might major in it or earn a certificate in it.

00:50:28.280 --> 00:50:29.660
and

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:33.800
see them again turning up in my courses or other courses. So I think it plays some role.

00:50:34.280 --> 00:50:40.700
And I think these students tend to fit a profile. And the profile is that the existing forms

00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:46.860
of Jewish identity, culture, and existing institutions that they know of, if not existing

00:50:46.860 --> 00:50:49.720
institutions than the ones they're most familiar with, the ones that have been part of their

00:50:49.720 --> 00:50:55.250
lives, they may have positive feelings, negative feelings, or somewhere in between

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:59.960
about them, but they don't excite them. And so that I think what they respect

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:09.220
respond to is that what Jewish studies does for them, I think, is give them a way of learning

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:15.500
about Jews in ways that are interesting. And that's what I think they respond to.

00:51:15.580 --> 00:51:21.340
It's interesting. It can be unconventional. Often it is, because the subject matter is

00:51:21.340 --> 00:51:27.380
not what they know. And they like it. They're curious about the Jewish past in some way.

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:29.980
and it provides them with them.

00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:33.920
not just information and knowledge,

00:51:33.940 --> 00:51:36.400
it expands their understanding of themselves

00:51:36.400 --> 00:51:38.680
and relationship to the Jews in the world.

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:42.980
And I think that's what they like about it.

00:51:43.140 --> 00:51:45.120
I don't know how many people I'm talking about.

00:51:45.460 --> 00:51:47.880
My own sense of it is maybe, I don't know,

00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:49.640
5% of the students I teach.

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:50.560
I'm not really sure.

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:54.320
But I know they're out there,

00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:55.660
and I think that's really,

00:51:56.600 --> 00:51:58.200
and even if it is just 5%,

00:51:58.200 --> 00:51:59.980
I don't think that's such a terrible...

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:08.000
number. You know, I think, I mean, excuse me, I don't know what, you know, I don't know how to

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:15.510
gauge it. I have no way of gauging how many people take, how many of my students will take Jewish

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:21.390
studies seriously. I don't know. I just know that there is some minority. It's a pretty decent

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:29.430
minority in my book. And without Jewish studies, I'm sure, I'm pretty sure they would have no

00:52:29.430 --> 00:52:29.970
interest in any.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:32.980
anything Jewish whatsoever. That's my feeling about it.

00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:35.120
I have two more questions, so we're running out of time.

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:40.820
What do you think the place of Yiddish is in universities and in the academy?

00:52:41.310 --> 00:52:50.780
Well, I think it's been growing slowly but surely, I think. Yiddish, the more, but we

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:58.120
need more Yiddish language instructors, we need more Yiddish studies programs, we need

00:52:58.120 --> 00:52:59.940
and more experts in Yiddish literature and culture.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:05.420
sure we just need more. I think the demand is there. I have no doubt about that. I'm

00:53:05.420 --> 00:53:14.140
really convinced of that. I'm convinced there's an unmet demand. And so it's been an increasingly,

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:18.640
I'd say sort of, if your question is descriptive, you know, what role has it played?

00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:19.340
Or should it play?

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:23.100
Or should, okay. So what role has it played? I'd say Yiddish over the last three to four

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:28.580
decades has become an increasingly important part of Jewish studies. You can see that by

00:53:28.580 --> 00:53:29.920
looking at course catalogs.

00:53:30.030 --> 00:53:34.530
programs at the Association for Jewish Studies, you can see that there's more and more people

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:41.650
studying Yiddish literature, language, culture. There are more doing it. The quality of the

00:53:41.650 --> 00:53:45.710
work and the quantity of the work just grows and grows and grows. What role should it play?

00:53:46.110 --> 00:53:53.790
It should play more of the same. So I think there should be more faculty hired, more

00:53:53.790 --> 00:53:59.870
programs created more money given to make these things happen and I would like

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:12.210
to see beyond that, I'd like to see in the academy and beyond efforts made to develop

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:18.260
Yiddish language fluency on some level. Fluency can mean different things, different ways

00:54:18.260 --> 00:54:26.560
of gauging fluency, so that Yiddish is part of the mainstream of American Jewish life.

00:54:26.680 --> 00:54:29.640
Not that the majority of Jews will be able to read it or speak it.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:31.400
That's probably unrealistic.

00:54:31.780 --> 00:54:35.780
But that an important minority will be able to read it and speak it,

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:42.640
and that all American Jews will regard Yiddish as central to American Jewish culture.

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:45.830
That, I think, should be the goal.

00:54:45.930 --> 00:54:47.450
I think that's actually a feasible goal.

00:54:47.690 --> 00:54:52.640
And Jewish studies probably stands at the center of that.

00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:59.610
If that were to be a project, Yiddish in the university would stand at the center of that project.

00:55:00.860 --> 00:55:03.020
Is there anything else that you'd like to talk about?

00:55:03.180 --> 00:55:04.020
I have one last question.

00:55:04.460 --> 00:55:04.860
No.

00:55:05.060 --> 00:55:05.240
Okay.
